- How would reconstruction of exact segments be implemented with explicit pointers?
- How would you handle varying campaign setup costs or penalties per segment length?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a constrained subsequence DP with gap bounds and reconstruction, a common real-world scheduling/selection pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on sequences with restricted gaps
- State dp[i][t] and transition over a window [i-U, i-L]
- Path reconstruction with parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, k, L, U){
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> Array(n).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[1][i] = A[i];
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best = NEG, bestj = -1;
      for(let j=i-U;j<=i-L;j++){
        if(j>=0 && dp[t-1][j] > best){ best = dp[t-1][j]; bestj = j; }
      }
      if(bestj!=-1){ dp[t][i] = A[i] + best; par[t][i] = bestj; }
    }
  }
  let end = -1, best = NEG;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[k][i] > best){ best = dp[k][i]; end = i; }
  const indices = [];
  let cur = end;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){ indices.push(cur); cur = par[t][cur]; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {best, indices};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model interval scheduling with a cap on concurrent starts, a common real-world constraint in job scheduling and ad campaigns.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a limit on number of intervals
- Preprocessing next[i] via binary search
- DP with reconstruction pointers for actual selection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch for dp and reconstruction
function maxProfitTasks(s, d, p, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const idx = [...Array(n).keys()].sort((a,b)=> s[a]-s[b]);
  // reorder s,d,p by idx as needed; compute next[] with binary search
  const next = Array(n).fill(n);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
    let limit = s[i] + d[i] - 1;
    // binary search for first j with s[j] > limit
    let lo=i+1, hi=n; while (lo<hi){
      let mid=(lo+hi)>>1; if (s[mid] > limit) hi=mid; else lo=mid+1; }
    next[i] = lo;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {
    for (let t=1;t<=k;t++) {
      if (dp[i+1][t] >= p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i+1][t];
        take[i][t] = false;
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {profit: dp[0][k], take, next};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow diagonal moves?
- How would you adapt for multiple starting points or 3D grids?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: parity constraint on subarrays adds a practical twist to classic DP without changing core technique.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Ending a subarray with even length using prefix sums
- Path reconstruction and memory optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEvenSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let l = 2; l <= i; l += 2) {
          const val = dp[i-l][t-1] + (S[i] - S[i-l]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            choice[i][t] = l;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct actual subarrays from the choice table?
- How to optimize to O(nk) time using parity-informed transitions?

## Why This Is Asked
New DP angle focusing on subarray range gain with exact k partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP partitioning
- Precomputing range max/min
- Reconstruction through pointers
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGainKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = A[l], mx = A[l];
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      gain[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      take[i][t] = take[i-1][t];
      if(t>0){
        for(let l=1;l<=i;l++){
          const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + gain[l-1][i-1];
          if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l-1; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const lminus1 = take[i][t];
    const l = lminus1 + 1;
    res.unshift([l, i]);
    i = lminus1; t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct blocks?
- How would the solution change if subarrays must have at least a fixed length L?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend DP with a cooldown constraint and to backtrack trades.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-state DP with cooldown
- 1-based transaction counting, backtracking
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length; if (!n || k===0) return 0;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  let no = new Array(k+1).fill(0);
  let hold = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  let cool = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  hold[0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i=1;i<n;i++){
    let nno=no.slice(), nhold=hold.slice(), ncool=cool.slice();
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      nno[t] = Math.max(nno[t], cool[t]);
      nhold[t] = Math.max(nhold[t], no[t] - prices[i]);
      if (t>0) ncool[t] = Math.max(ncool[t], hold[t-1] + prices[i]);
    }
    no=nno; hold=nhold; cool=ncool;
  }
  let best=0; for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) best=Math.max(best,no[t],cool[t]);
  return best;
}
```

