- What memory optimizations can reduce the space complexity from O(nk)?
- How would the solution change if the blocks could be non-contiguous?
- Can you propose a divide-and-conquer optimization to improve the time complexity?

## Why This Problem Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming on partitioning with a non-linear cost function (block maximum). It extends beyond standard max-sum variants and evaluates careful indexing, prefix handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- **Contiguous partition DP**: State dp[i][t] represents the minimum cost for the prefix of length i using exactly t blocks
- **Transition**: Computes the running maximum for the last block while finding the optimal split point
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking through the DP table to determine block endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumOfBlockMax(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const split = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      let maxVal = 0;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= t - 1; j--) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
        const cost = dp[j][t - 1] + maxVal;
        if (cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cost;
          split[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = split[i][t];
    blocks.unshift({ start: j + 1, end: i });
    i = j;
    t--;
  }

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

